/-!
# The support is the closure of the associated points, and germs along a specialization

Let `X` be a locally Noetherian scheme and `F` a quasi-coherent `𝒪_X`-module.

The first half of this file identifies the support of `F` with the closure of its set of
associated points when `F` is moreover of finite type. One inclusion is that an associated
point lies in the support together with the closedness of the support; the other is local on an
affine open `W`, where the support of `F` is the zero locus of the annihilator of `Γ(F, W)` and
a minimal prime of that annihilator is an associated prime.

The second half is the vanishing criterion the algebraization statements of Stacks chapter 52
consume. Germ vanishing propagates along a specialization, from a point to the points it is a
specialization of, because the stalk at the more generic point is a quotient of the stalk at
the more special one through `TopCat.Presheaf.stalkSpecializes`. Conversely a section of `F`
over *any* open whose germ vanishes at every associated point of `F` is zero, which generalizes
the injectivity of restriction of Stacks tag 0B3L from the maximal open to an arbitrary pair of
opens.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_mem_associatedPrimes_annihilator_le` — over a Noetherian ring, a
  nonzero element of a module has its annihilator contained in some associated prime of the
  module.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.specializes_of_primeIdealOf_le` — on an affine open, containment of the
  corresponding primes is a specialization.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.supp_eq_closure_associatedPoints` — the support of a quasi-coherent module
  of finite type on a locally Noetherian scheme is the closure of its associated points.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.germ_eq_zero_of_specializes` — germ vanishing transports along a
  specialization.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.eq_zero_of_forall_germ_associatedPoints_eq_zero` — a section over an open
  whose germ vanishes at every associated point is zero.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.restriction_injective_of_associatedPoints_inter_subset` — restriction
  between two opens is injective as soon as the smaller one contains every associated point of
  the larger one.

## Implementation notes

The transport of germ vanishing needs only the canonical map `F_y ⟶ F_x` attached to a
specialization `x ⤳ y`, not the finer fact that `F_x` is a localization of `F_y`: the image of
`0` is `0`. So no localization statement is used, and the lemma holds for an arbitrary sheaf of
modules, with no quasi-coherence and no Noetherian hypothesis.

The algebraic input to the vanishing criterion is applied to the cyclic submodule generated by
the section, which is a finite module whatever `F` is; that is why neither
`AlgebraicGeometry.eq_zero_of_forall_germ_associatedPoints_eq_zero` nor
`AlgebraicGeometry.restriction_injective_of_associatedPoints_inter_subset` asks `F` to be of
finite type, matching the hypotheses of the maximal-open case
`AlgebraicGeometry.restriction_injective_of_associatedPoints_subset`.

Germs are written `F.presheaf.germ`, the spelling the consumers use, and the lemmas of
`Mathlib.Topology.Sheaves.Stalks` are applied to `F.val.presheaf`; the two are definitionally
equal but not interchangeable by `rw`, so every step crossing between them is in term mode.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 05AD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05AD)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05AG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05AG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05AH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05AH)
